The proposed architecture looks good, but before implementation make the following adjustments:

1. Do not treat dependency_correlated_failure as proof that a specific dependency such as Kafka or DB is down unless direct evidence exists. Use evidence-based language such as possible_shared_dependency_correlation when only workload behavior supports the inference.
2. Do not use 200 observations as the sole ML readiness criterion. Require minimum observations plus feature completeness, temporal coverage, non-degenerate variance, and other appropriate data-quality checks.
3. Design hierarchical ML fallback: workload-level model → namespace-level model → statistical baseline → deterministic rules, depending on available history.
4. Add temporal/seasonal baseline features such as hour-of-day and day-of-week where enough history exists.
5. Add change-point detection/change-intensity analysis as part of the time-series layer.
6. Treat the evidence model as a first-class component. Every non-trivial insight should retain the signals, observations, temporal relationships, baseline comparisons, and correlation evidence that contributed to it.
7. Keep raw observations, derived features, analytical findings, and final insights logically separate.
8. Implement the foundation and data-quality layer before enabling ML.
9. Do not change existing dashboard job behavior.
10. Do not add node, HPA, PVC, or cluster-wide analytics because those are outside the available access/use case.

Before writing implementation code, update the architecture/design assessment with these changes and present the final phased implementation plan.
